---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import {
  Chips,
  Eyebrow,
  Grid,
  Head,
  Heading,
  HeroCTAs,
  HeroStack,
  Lede,
  Links,
  P,
  PageHero,
  RuledCard,
  Sec,
  Stack,
  StackArrow,
  StackBox,
  StackNote,
  StackPanel,
  Two,
} from '../../components/ds';
import { INNER_CTAS } from '../../data/site';

const PROTOCOLS = ['pgwire', 'Bolt', 'Arrow Flight', 'gRPC', 'JDBC', 'REST', 'WebSocket', 'Airport', 'MCP'];
---

<BaseLayout
  title="Query interfaces & wire protocols — Provisa"
  description="Three query languages and nine wire protocols over one federated model: GraphQL, SQL, Cypher over pgwire, Bolt, Arrow Flight, gRPC, JDBC, REST, WebSocket, Airport, and MCP."
  active="Interfaces"
>
  <PageHero crumb="Interfaces">
    <Eyebrow tone="cobalt">Query interfaces &amp; wire protocols</Eyebrow>
    <Heading level="page">Three languages. Nine protocols. One model.</Heading>
    <Lede>
      The query language and the wire protocol are independent choices. Every entry point resolves against the same federated model and passes
      through the same governance — there is no path in that skips it.
    </Lede>
    <P>
      One model does not mean one namespace. You divide sources into <strong>domains</strong>, and a domain is what a SQL client sees as a schema —
      so <code>sales.orders</code> and <code>finance.orders</code> stay distinct while resolving through the same governed model.
    </P>
    <HeroCTAs primary={INNER_CTAS.primary} secondary={INNER_CTAS.secondary} />
    <HeroStack slot="aside" label="Three languages over nine protocols, one model">
      <StackBox label="QUERY LANGUAGES">GraphQL · SQL · Cypher</StackBox>
      <StackArrow />
      <StackPanel label="WIRE PROTOCOLS · NINE">
        <Chips items={PROTOCOLS} />
      </StackPanel>
      <StackNote label="ONE MODEL">Same compiler, same six governance layers</StackNote>
    </HeroStack>
  </PageHero>

  <Sec band="white" labelledBy="languages-title">
    <Stack gap={40}>
      <Heading level="section-sm" id="languages-title">Query languages</Heading>
      <Grid min={280} gap={40}>
        <RuledCard rule="cobalt" weight={4} titleSize={23} title="GraphQL">
          <p>
            Per-role schemas with field-level visibility, cursor pagination, and aggregates. The schema is constrained to registered relationships,
            so a query that traverses an unapproved path cannot be expressed — it is <em>valid by construction</em> rather than rejected at runtime.
            Apollo Automatic Persisted Queries are included.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard rule="forest" weight={4} titleSize={23} title="SQL">
          <p>
            Full SQL over federated data, correlated subqueries and all. Cross-source joins fan out through the federation engine; single-source
            queries bypass federation entirely and route direct to the driver, transactional and sub-100ms.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard rule="vermilion" weight={4} titleSize={23} title="Cypher">
          <p>
            Graph queries over the same federated model. Traverse relationships as edges, union sources into a single node label, and walk
            variable-length paths — under identical governance to the SQL and GraphQL paths.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
      </Grid>
    </Stack>
  </Sec>

  <Sec band="forest" labelledBy="protocols-title">
    <Stack gap={40}>
      <Heading level="section-sm" id="protocols-title">Wire protocols</Heading>
      <Grid min={300} gap={40}>
        <RuledCard title="pgwire — port 5439">
          <p>
            Any Postgres client connects: psql, DBeaver, DataGrip, pandas <code>read_sql</code>, or anything else speaking the frontend/backend
            protocol. <code>pg_catalog</code> is answered in-memory, so schema browsers, foreign-key arrows, and autocomplete work without a real
            Postgres behind them.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="Bolt (Neo4j)">
          <p>
            Neo4j Browser, Bloom, and the official drivers run Cypher over Bolt against the federated graph. Each role surfaces as a <code
              >provisa_&lt;role&gt;</code
            > database, so the client's database picker is the governance boundary.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="Arrow Flight">
          <p>
            Arrow record batches streamed over gRPC — columnar all the way out, with no row-by-row serialization between the engine and the client.
            Accepts GraphQL or SQL. Result sets are unbounded: nothing is materialized server-side while the client reads.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="gRPC model API">
          <p>
            A <code>.proto</code> is generated from the registered schema: typed query and insert RPCs per table, with streamed responses. Regenerating
            is a consequence of changing the model, not a separate build step.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="JDBC, REST, and JSON:API">
          <p>
            BI tools — Tableau, Power BI, DBeaver — connect over JDBC. Applications get two HTTP shapes over the same model: plain REST at <code
              >/data/rest/{'{table}'}</code
            >, with filter, ordering, pagination, and field selection as query parameters, and JSON:API 1.1 at <code>/data/jsonapi/{'{table}'}</code> with
            sparse fieldsets and filter expressions. Both are described by a generated OpenAPI 3.1 document, so a client generator produces a typed SDK
            without anyone hand-writing a spec.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="Airport (DuckDB)">
          <p>
            Any DuckDB client attaches Provisa as a database — <code>ATTACH 'grpc://host' AS p (TYPE AIRPORT)</code> — and queries the governed federated
            model from local DuckDB. Filters and projections push down; writes update by primary key.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard title="WebSocket and SSE">
          <p>
            Subscriptions stream near-real-time change events. The backend is your choice: Postgres native notification, MongoDB change streams, CDC,
            or polling — and events can fan out over Kafka.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
      </Grid>
    </Stack>
  </Sec>

  <Sec labelledBy="agents-title">
    <Two>
      <Head>
        <Eyebrow>AI &amp; automation</Eyebrow>
        <Heading level="section-sm" id="agents-title">For AI agents</Heading>
      </Head>
      <Stack gap={40}>
        <RuledCard rule="vermilion" weight={4} titleSize={23} title="MCP server">
          <p>
            A Model Context Protocol server — stdio and remote Streamable HTTP — exposes your governed, federated data to agents like Claude as tools.
            The OAuth token maps to a role, so every agent call is governed by the same six layers as a human query. Agents get no bypass, and no
            separate policy to keep in sync.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <RuledCard rule="vermilion" weight={4} titleSize={23} title="Natural language">
          <p>
            NL→SQL, Cypher, or GraphQL powered by Claude, with an interactive validation loop so a generated query is reviewed before anything
            executes.
          </p>
        </RuledCard>
        <Links
          links={[
            { label: 'How governance applies to every one of them', href: '/why/governance' },
            { label: 'Download Provisa', href: '/#download' },
          ]}
        />
      </Stack>
    </Two>
  </Sec>
</BaseLayout>
